Deep Talent Formation and the Research-First University Architecture: A Systems Framework for Strategic National Capability Building in Morocco

ouadi Maakoul 


ABSTRACT

Morocco confronts a structural paradox at the heart of its national development ambition: graduate production is rising while strategic innovation capacity stagnates. This thesis argues that the root cause is not a failure of access or enrollment, but a failure of competence architecture — the deep systemic mechanisms through which educational institutions transform students into high-capacity builders, researchers, and problem solvers capable of advancing sovereign national systems. As of 2023, graduate unemployment in Morocco reached 25.9%, nearly twice the national average of 13%, while the economy shed 157,000 positions in that year alone. Morocco's total research and development expenditure hovers at only 0.75% of GDP, far below the world average of 2.68% and the European average of 2.24%.

Drawing on systems engineering theory, human capital economics, national innovation systems literature, and complexity science, this thesis introduces the Competence Conversion Model (CCM) as a theoretical framework for diagnosing and redesigning higher education systems in emerging economies with strategic development ambitions.

The research proposes, operationalizes, and defends the concept of a Research-First University Architecture (RFUA) — a structural blueprint for institutions designed explicitly around deep competence formation rather than credential distribution. The RFUA integrates four interdependent pillars: educational rigor and epistemic culture, research integration from the earliest academic years, practical execution through national-mission-aligned projects, and strategic sectoral alignment across AI, aerospace, energy, advanced manufacturing, and robotics. This architecture directly addresses findings that over 50% of Moroccan university students are enrolled in fields such as humanities and social sciences, which offer limited job prospects, while growing sectors like IT, automotive, and engineering remain acutely undersupplied with talent.

An AI-Augmented Talent Formation System is further proposed as the operational layer of the RFUA, enabling personalized competence tracking, mentor-project matching, simulation-based learning environments, and a National Knowledge Graph linking research gaps, industrial needs, and university output.

The thesis concludes with a Morocco 2040 Deep Tech Talent Pipeline — a phased national roadmap translating the RFUA framework into institutional policy, investment architecture, and measurable national scientific capability indices. This roadmap is calibrated against Morocco’s New Development Model (NMD), which defines four principal axes of transformation targeting sustainable economic, human, social, and territorial progress by 2035, and the "Maroc Digital 2030" strategy aiming to create hundreds of thousands of jobs in the tech sector and train 200,000 AI-skilled graduates by 2030.

Keywords: Higher Education Reform, National Innovation Systems, Talent Formation, Research-First Architecture, Human Capital, Morocco, AI in Education, Strategic Sovereignty, Deep Tech, Competence Systems

