CHAPTER 2: LITERATURE REVIEW

---

2.1 National Innovation Systems Theory

The concept of the National Innovation System (NIS) was developed concurrently by Freeman (1987), Lundvall (1992), and Nelson (1993), each approaching from distinct but complementary angles. Freeman's foundational work on Japan's industrial rise demonstrated that nations which invested systematically in knowledge creation, institutional learning, and industry-university linkages achieved sustained technological leadership that could not be explained by resource endowment or labor cost alone.

Lundvall's contribution introduced the centrality of interactive learning to innovation — the idea that knowledge creation is not a linear pipeline from research to application, but a complex, bidirectional system of interactions between firms, universities, government institutions, and users. This insight is foundational to the RFUA framework developed in this thesis: if knowledge creation is inherently interactive, then universities that operate in isolation from industry, government missions, and research ecosystems are structurally incapable of producing genuinely innovative talent.

Nelson's comparative analysis of national innovation systems across fifteen countries demonstrated the enormous variance in outcomes produced by different institutional arrangements — and, critically, that these differences were not primarily a function of spending levels but of systemic design: how research is organized, how universities relate to industry, how talent is trained, and how knowledge flows between institutions. Recent scholarship has extended this analysis to developing countries, providing a conceptual model for conducting NIS studies in developing economies and critically examining whether the NIS framework, originally developed for advanced economies, adequately captures the innovation dynamics of emerging nations.

For Morocco, NIS theory provides an immediate analytical lens. Morocco's innovation system, insofar as one can be said to exist as an integrated system, is characterized by weak institutional coupling: universities, research centers, strategic industries, and national development agencies operate largely in parallel rather than in dynamic interaction. The CESE report of 2025 confirmed that "universities and scientific institutions have expanded in recent years, and Morocco’s indexed scientific production has tripled over the course of a decade. But there is no coordination of the public, academic, and private sectors, an out-of-date legal framework, and insufficient incentives for companies to invest in innovation". The RFUA framework proposed in this thesis represents, in essence, a national strategy for rebuilding the linkage architecture of Morocco's innovation system from the university outward.

2.2 Human Capital Theory and Its Limits

Gary Becker's seminal work on human capital (1964) established the economic case for investment in education and training as a form of capital accumulation. Schultz (1961) had earlier articulated the same insight at the national level, arguing that education was the primary driver of the productivity gains that standard factor inputs — land, labor, physical capital — could not explain.

The human capital framework has been enormously influential in development economics and education policy. However, it carries a critical limitation that is directly relevant to the problem this thesis addresses: it treats education as homogeneous. A year of schooling, in the standard Mincerian framework, is a year of schooling — it adds to human capital stock regardless of what is actually learned, how deeply it is understood, or whether it equips its recipient to perform any specific productive function.

This limitation has produced a generation of education policy focused on enrollment, years of schooling, and credential attainment as proxies for human capital, rather than on the much harder to measure but far more consequential question of competence depth — what individuals can actually do with what they have supposedly learned.

Hanushek and Woessmann's landmark contributions to this debate (2008, 2015, 2023) demonstrated, using international cognitive skill assessments across decades, that it is not years of schooling but measurable cognitive skill — particularly in mathematics and science — that drives economic growth. Their finding that the cognitive skill of a nation's population explains a substantially larger share of long-run growth variation than years of schooling alone is, for the purposes of this thesis, a foundational empirical anchor. In their 2023 volume, they subject their hypotheses about the relationship between cognitive skills and economic growth to a series of tests, including alternate specifications, different subsets of countries, and econometric analysis of causal interpretations.

It implies that Morocco's challenge is not one of enrolling more students or issuing more degrees, but of fundamentally increasing the depth of genuine competence that its educational system produces. The skills gap in Morocco is well documented: 44% of Moroccan firms report difficulties finding workers with appropriate technical skills, with the gap most evident in sectors demanding specialized expertise such as engineering, IT, and manufacturing.

2.3 Research Universities and National Development

The history of national development in the twentieth century is inseparable from the history of research universities. The United States' emergence as the dominant global technological power in the post-war era was built substantially on a deliberate national strategy of investing in research universities as engines of frontier knowledge creation — a strategy articulated in Vannevar Bush's landmark 1945 report Science: The Endless Frontier.

The American research university model — characterized by graduate education embedded in active research programs, faculty who are practicing researchers rather than merely lecturers, strong industry linkages, and government funding of basic research — became the global template for knowledge-economy institution building.

Germany's Technische Hochschule tradition, from which the modern technical university descends, offers an earlier and in some ways more instructive model for emerging industrial nations: institutions designed explicitly around the integration of science and engineering practice, producing graduates capable not merely of applying known techniques but of developing new ones.

South Korea's transformation from a war-devastated agrarian economy to a global technology leader within two generations — producing Samsung, Hyundai, POSCO, and a vibrant semiconductor industry — was built in part on a deliberate national strategy of building world-class engineering and science universities and coupling them tightly to national industrial champions. In the early stage of industrial development during the 1960s and 1980s, government research institutes were direct and effective tools for nurturing S&T manpower, while since the 1990s, the importance of university research has increased, with the government fostering graduate research manpower through the research-oriented university policy of the BK21 program.

China’s Project 985, launched in 1998, sought to foster a belief in the importance of innovation among students of 39 partner universities, with Tsinghua University and Peking University each receiving the equivalent of nearly $276 million. Research found that students graduating from Project 985 universities were significantly more likely to create innovative, high-tech firms. The Double First-Class Plan, which replaced Project 985/211, included 137 universities and 465 disciplines in its first round, with over $14.14 billion spent from 2018 to 2020.

The common thread across these cases, and the central lesson for Morocco, is that research universities capable of producing frontier talent do not emerge organically from general education expansion. They require deliberate architectural design, sustained investment, and strategic coupling to national development priorities.

2.4 The Talent Formation Literature

The psychology of expertise development — most influentially articulated by Anders Ericsson in his theory of deliberate practice (1993, 2016) — provides important microfoundations for the competence formation model advanced in this thesis. Ericsson’s research across domains from chess to surgery to music demonstrated that genuine expertise — the capacity to perform at the frontier of a field — is not primarily a function of innate talent or general intelligence, but of structured, effortful practice focused on specific skill development under expert feedback. While the deliberate practice view has faced critical scrutiny regarding its definitional consistency and evidentiary standards over more than 25 years of scholarly debate, it remains a highly influential perspective in the literature on expertise.

The implications for educational design are profound. If deep competence is the product of deliberate practice under expert guidance rather than exposure to information, then lecture-centered, examination-focused educational architectures are structurally misaligned with competence formation. They produce students who have been exposed to information and can reproduce it under test conditions — but who lack the practiced, embodied capacity to apply knowledge to novel, complex, high-stakes problems.

This insight is reinforced by the literature on project-based learning and problem-based learning in engineering education (Dym et al., 2005; Prince & Felder, 2006), which consistently demonstrates superior competence outcomes — not just higher student engagement — when learning is organized around authentic, complex problems requiring integration across disciplines.

Carol Dweck's research on growth mindset (2006) adds a motivational dimension: the belief that competence is developable through effort, rather than fixed by innate ability, is itself a predictor of learning depth and persistence. Recent scholarship, including a 2020 article in American Psychologist, has sought to answer questions about whether growth mindset predicts student outcomes and whether growth mindset interventions work reliably, finding nuanced but supportive evidence. Educational systems that operate on implicit fixed-mindset assumptions — sorting students early, rewarding reproduction over inquiry, punishing failure rather than treating it as information — actively suppress the motivational architecture required for deep learning.

2.5 Brain Drain, Brain Gain, and Talent Retention

The emigration of highly educated talent from developing countries — brain drain — has been extensively studied (Docquier & Rapoport, 2012; Stark et al., 1997). Morocco is a significant exporter of skilled emigrants, particularly to France, Spain, and Belgium, with substantial flows also to Canada, Germany, and the Gulf states. The phenomenon has been described as a covert strategy driving reform in certain sectors, as medical professionals migrate to Europe for better opportunities, harming Morocco's development strategy.

The standard economic framework treats brain drain as an unambiguous welfare loss for sending countries. However, the diaspora economics literature (Saxenian, 2006; Kerr, 2008) has complicated this picture, demonstrating that under certain institutional conditions, skilled emigrants create network externalities — knowledge transfer, investment, remittances, co-founding of firms — that partially offset or even reverse the welfare loss. Saxenian’s concept of "the New Argonauts" — foreign-born, technically skilled entrepreneurs who travel back and forth between Silicon Valley and their home countries — provides a framework for understanding how diaspora talent can be mobilized for national development rather than lost to it.

For Morocco, the relevant question is not merely how to retain graduates, but how to create the conditions — research excellence, frontier projects, competitive compensation, national mission meaning — under which Morocco's most capable individuals would choose to build their careers domestically rather than abroad. This is, in essence, a question of building institutions and missions compelling enough to compete with the gravitational pull of European research universities and industrial firms. Morocco’s Ambassador to the United States, Youssef Amrani, has explicitly highlighted that Moroccan expatriates represent a strategic asset, capable of mentoring, co-investing, and supporting projects that connect skills and opportunities across continents.

The RFUA framework, by creating institutions where genuine frontier research is conducted and where national strategic projects of real consequence are pursued, directly addresses the structural conditions that drive brain drain: the absence of sufficiently compelling professional opportunities for deep talent at home.

2.6 AI and the Future of Education

The emergence of large-scale AI systems capable of personalized tutoring, knowledge synthesis, research assistance, and adaptive learning creates new possibilities for educational architecture that were not available to earlier reformers.

The literature on intelligent tutoring systems (Koedinger & Corbett, 2006; VanLehn, 2011) demonstrates consistent learning gains from AI-mediated personalized instruction relative to traditional classroom settings — in some studies, effect sizes approaching two standard deviations. More recent work on large language models in educational contexts suggests the emergence of genuinely capable AI research and learning partners that can provide expert-level feedback across a wide range of technical domains. Intelligent tutoring systems provide adaptive and customized support, fostering enhanced student engagement and performance.

Recent research has introduced a novel methodology for enhancing intelligent tutoring systems through the integration of generative artificial intelligence and specialized AI agents, implementing a dual-layer GenAI validation approach that utilizes multiple large language models to ensure the reliability and pedagogical integrity of AI-generated content. Studies such as SmartTutor AI demonstrate curriculum-aligned adaptive tutoring systems that integrate learner modeling, knowledge tracing, adaptive difficulty selection, and closed-loop assessment within a modular architecture, treating generative AI as a constrained instructional component rather than an open-ended tutor.

An exploratory randomized controlled trial in UK secondary school classrooms evaluated whether an AI tutor can safely and effectively support students, reporting promising results that suggest AI tutoring can indeed provide effective personalized support at scale.

The critical insight for the present thesis is that AI does not merely accelerate existing educational models — it enables entirely new architectures of learning that would be impossible at scale without AI. Personalized competence mapping, adaptive research mentorship, simulation-based engineering laboratories, and national knowledge graphs linking talent development to strategic sector needs are all architecturally feasible only with AI as the operational substrate.

