CHAPTER 4: RESEARCH METHODOLOGY

---

4.1 Methodological Philosophy

This thesis is located at the intersection of education policy research, systems engineering, and national development strategy. It draws on multiple methodological traditions, arguing that the complexity of the phenomenon under study — the systemic failure of a national talent formation architecture — demands methodological pluralism rather than adherence to any single research paradigm.

The primary methodological orientation is critical systems inquiry (Midgley, 2000; Jackson, 2003): an approach that treats social systems as complex, historically situated, and amenable to both diagnosis and redesign through the application of rigorous theoretical frameworks. Critical systems inquiry combines the explanatory ambitions of social science with the design ambitions of engineering, seeking not merely to understand why systems fail but to construct frameworks for building better ones.

4.2 Methods

Comparative Case Analysis forms the empirical backbone of Chapter 5's diagnosis of Morocco's higher education system. Case analysis draws on published data from Morocco's Ministry of National Education, UNESCO Institute for Statistics, World Bank education data, reports from the Economic, Social, and Environmental Council (CESE, 2025), the High Commission for Planning (HCP, 2023-2024), and academic studies of Moroccan higher education and labor markets.

Comparative reference cases — South Korea's university transformation strategy (1960s–1990s), Singapore's deliberate research university construction (1990s–2010s), China's Project 985 elite university initiative, and Israel's military-linked deep-tech talent pipeline — provide benchmarks against which Morocco's system design and performance can be evaluated. Additional analytical leverage is drawn from the Israel Defense Forces’ Unit 8200, which has emerged as a significant talent pipeline for Silicon Valley, with graduates founding cybersecurity start-ups collectively valued at around $160 billion in publicly traded companies alone.

Framework Development is the primary theoretical contribution of the thesis. The CCM, the RFUA blueprint, the AI-Augmented Talent Formation System design, and the NSCI are all theoretical constructs developed through the synthesis of existing literature with the analytical results of the comparative diagnosis.

Systems Modeling is used to formalize the dynamic relationships within the CCM, the RFUA, and the national talent pipeline. Systems modeling allows the identification of feedback loops, leverage points, and potential failure modes in proposed institutional architectures before they are implemented.

Policy Analysis and Roadmap Development translates the theoretical framework and institutional architecture into actionable national policy proposals in Chapter 8, drawing on implementation science literature to address the practical constraints of institutional reform in the Moroccan political economy. Morocco's ongoing higher education reform, including the refoundation of the pedagogical architecture for license and master cycles through ministerial decrees signed in July 2025 and the establishment of 26 new universities, provides a dynamic policy context for this analysis.

4.3 Theoretical Validity

The theoretical frameworks advanced in this thesis are validated through three complementary mechanisms: internal logical consistency (do the arguments cohere?), empirical grounding (are the frameworks anchored in documented patterns of evidence?), and comparative plausibility (do the proposed frameworks explain variation in outcomes across the comparative reference cases?).

