CHAPTER 8: MOROCCO 2040 — THE DEEP TECH TALENT PIPELINE

---

8.1 The Strategic Context

Morocco's New Development Model (2021) articulates an ambition for economic transformation centered on high-value sectors, national sovereignty in critical technologies, and the development of a knowledge-based economy. The NMD’s four principal axes of transformation target sustainable progress at the economic, human, social, and territorial levels by 2035, and the reforms and projects proposed require additional public funding of 4% of GDP annually in the start-up phase (2022-2025) and 10% of GDP at cruising speed by 2030.

These ambitions are credible only if Morocco can build the sovereign talent base required to execute them. The government is prioritizing investment in strategic sectors including electronics, automotive, aviation, artificial intelligence, renewable energy, green hydrogen, and biotechnology. The “Maroc Digital 2030” strategy aims to create hundreds of thousands of jobs in the tech sector and train 200,000 AI-skilled graduates by 2030. Morocco’s Prime Minister has stated that the country has "a real potential in multiple areas which allows a strong ambition by 2035".

Yet the talent supply gap is quantifiable and severe: universities in Morocco produce fewer than 150 mechatronics graduates annually against demand for 1,800 specialists by 2030; the automotive sector faces a projected shortfall of 15,000 skilled workers; and 44% of Moroccan firms report difficulty finding workers with appropriate technical skills.

The Deep Tech Talent Pipeline proposed in this chapter translates the RFUA framework and AATFS system into a phased national implementation roadmap aligned with Morocco's 2040 strategic horizon.

8.2 Phase 1 (2026–2029): Foundation Architecture

Objective: Establish the institutional, policy, and technological foundations of the RFUA and AATFS.

The first phase focuses on the establishment of a National Talent Formation Authority (NTFA) — a strategic agency with the mandate and institutional authority to coordinate talent formation across universities, industry, research centers, and government agencies. The NTFA is the governance infrastructure without which coordination failures will prevent the RFUA from reaching its potential. Its establishment would directly address the CESE’s finding that "there is no coordination of the public, academic, and private sectors" in Morocco’s current research and innovation landscape.

Simultaneously, Phase 1 establishes the first cohort of Pilot RFUA Institutions — two to three existing institutions redesigned around RFUA principles, with priority given to institutions already demonstrating research capacity: EMI, ENSAM Casablanca, and the Mohammed VI Polytechnic University (UM6P). UM6P has already established over 200 partnerships with universities, research centers, and industrial actors and launched an AI Lab in partnership with Syensqo to bridge academic research and industrial application. These institutions receive priority investment in faculty recruitment (particularly from the Moroccan diaspora), Mission Laboratory establishment, and AATFS infrastructure deployment.

The first version of the National Knowledge Graph is developed in Phase 1, integrating existing data on university output, industrial sector needs, and national research capacity into a coherent strategic intelligence system.

Phase 1 Success Metrics: Three RFUA pilot institutions operational; 50 diaspora researcher faculty recruited; 10 Mission Laboratories established; first AATFS PCM system deployed; NSCI baseline established.

8.3 Phase 2 (2029–2033): System Expansion

Objective: Scale the RFUA model across Morocco's higher education system, with full AATFS integration.

Phase 2 expands the RFUA model to a national network of institutions, with differentiation by strategic domain: specific institutions develop deep expertise in AI and data systems, others in aerospace and advanced manufacturing, others in energy systems, others in biotechnology and life sciences. This phase aligns with Morocco’s ongoing expansion, including the establishment of 26 new universities in the first phase of national reforms and 23 more in the second phase.

The National Mission Immersion program reaches full operational scale, with thousands of students per year engaging in structured immersions with national strategic partners. The Research Mentor Intelligence system is fully deployed, optimizing mentor-project matching at national scale.

International partnerships are formalized with frontier research universities — targeting joint doctoral programs, co-supervised research, and collaborative Mission Laboratory projects with institutions in Germany, South Korea, Japan, Canada, and the United States. Morocco’s investment in digital infrastructure provides a foundation for these partnerships.

Phase 2 Success Metrics: 20 RFUA institutions operational; NSCI improvement of 40% from baseline; Morocco's national research output doubles from Phase 1 baseline; brain drain rate among top-quartile graduates reduces by 30%.

8.4 Phase 3 (2033–2040): Sovereign Capability Achievement

Objective: Morocco achieves sovereign capability in targeted deep-tech domains, with indigenous capacity for design, development, and production of frontier technology systems.

By 2040, the goal is not merely a better educated workforce. The goal is a Morocco that builds its own AI systems rather than deploying imported ones; that designs and manufactures components of its own aerospace and defense systems; that develops original solutions to its energy transition challenges rather than purchasing technology from abroad; and that contributes original research to the global frontier of knowledge in domains of national strategic importance.

Phase 3 achievement requires that the first cohort of RFUA graduates — those who entered the system in 2026 — have completed doctoral formation, accumulated five to ten years of post-doctoral research or industry experience, and returned to or remained in Morocco as the founding generation of the country's sovereign deep-tech capability. Morocco aims for R&D expenditure to reach 1.5% of GDP by 2040, converging toward the levels of Turkey, Spain, and Malaysia.

Phase 3 Success Metrics: Morocco ranked among the top 50 nations in selected NSCI domains; at least three nationally developed technology systems deployed at national infrastructure scale; Morocco's per-capita research output reaches the median of upper-middle-income comparators; net brain gain (returns exceeding exits) achieved among top-quartile technical talent.

8.5 Investment Architecture

The RFUA and AATFS require sustained, long-term investment at a scale commensurate with their strategic importance. This thesis proposes a Triple Investment Model drawing on three complementary funding streams.

The first stream is national public investment — a dedicated national strategic talent fund, established by parliamentary mandate with multi-year funding commitments protected from annual budget cycles. This institutional protection of long-term investment is modeled on South Korea's Science and Technology Basic Plans, which provided sustained funding across political cycles, including the Special Act on Support for Scientists and Engineers and its regularly revised Master Plans that have been essential tools in systematically managing science and technology manpower policies.

The second stream is sovereign strategic industry contribution — mandatory research and talent development contributions from national strategic companies (OCP, ONEE, Royal Air Maroc, and national defense industry partners) as part of their sovereign mandate and licensing arrangements. This addresses the CESE’s finding that private sector contributions to research funding are estimated at less than 30%.

The third stream is international partnership funding — grants and co-investment from international development finance institutions (World Bank, African Development Bank, European Investment Bank), strategic bilateral partners, and diaspora philanthropy.

