APPENDIX A — THE COMPETENCE CONVERSION MODEL: VARIABLE CALIBRATION PROTOCOL

This appendix provides the methodology for operationalizing and calibrating the four variables of the Competence Conversion Model (CCM) — Educational Rigor (E), Research Integration (R), Practical Execution (P), and Strategic Alignment (A) — through the National Scientific Capability Index (NSCI). The protocol translates theoretical constructs into measurable indicators, data sources, and aggregation procedures suitable for national-level assessment and international benchmarking.

A.1 Indicator Selection and Data Sources

Each CCM variable is decomposed into a set of quantifiable indicators drawn from multiple data streams: national education statistics, institutional performance data, standardized assessments, industry surveys, patent and publication databases, and international benchmarking instruments.

E_index Indicators

· E1: Mean performance on a nationally administered deep conceptual understanding test in core STEM disciplines (mathematics, physics, computer science), scaled 0–100.
· E2: Percentage of faculty holding a PhD from a globally ranked research university (top 500) or with an active international publication record (≥2 peer-reviewed articles in the past five years).
· E3: Curriculum rigor index, measured through international accreditation status (e.g., ABET, EUR-ACE) and credit-hour alignment with European Credit Transfer System (ECTS) benchmarks for comparable programs.
· E4: Student-reported epistemic culture index, derived from annual surveys measuring agreement with statements such as "My professors reward original thinking over memorization" and "I am encouraged to question established theories."

Data sources: National Center for Educational Evaluation and Examinations (hypothetical or adapted from existing CNEF), Ministry of Higher Education administrative records, Scopus/Web of Science publication databases, and institutional self-studies.

R_index Indicators

· R1: Research publications per FTE faculty per year, weighted by journal impact factor (normalized to field).
· R2: Percentage of undergraduate students who have co-authored at least one peer-reviewed publication or conference paper before graduation.
· R3: National research expenditure as a percentage of GDP (disaggregated by source: government, industry, international).
· R4: Number of active national/international patent applications (PCT) per million population originating from university-affiliated inventors.
· R5: Survey-based measure of the proportion of students reporting "frequent engagement in research activities as part of coursework" on a 5-point Likert scale.

Data sources: UNESCO Institute for Statistics, WIPO IP Statistics, national R&D surveys, institutional research offices, and student exit surveys.

P_index Indicators

· P1: Capstone project complexity rating, assessed by an independent industry-academic panel using a standardized rubric (scale 1–5) evaluating problem novelty, multidisciplinary integration, and real-world constraint handling.
· P2: Percentage of graduates who complete at least one industry-based immersion or cooperative education placement of ≥16 weeks.
· P3: Graduate employment rate in engineering/technology roles requiring "design and development" rather than "operation or maintenance" at 12 months post-graduation (as per labor force survey definitions).
· P4: Employer satisfaction score with graduate practical execution capability (annual survey, 1–10 scale).

Data sources: Institutional career services, national employment observatory, industry association surveys (CGEM, AMICA, GIMAS), and capstone project databases.

A_index Indicators

· A1: Proportion of graduates entering employment in nationally designated strategic sectors (aerospace, automotive, AI/digital, renewable energy, advanced manufacturing, biotechnology) within 24 months.
· A2: Percentage of university research output tagged as aligned with national strategic priority areas (as defined by the National Talent Formation Authority).
· A3: Industry co-funding of university research as a share of total university research budget.
· A4: Number of formal strategic partnership agreements between universities and national flagship industrial or defense entities.

Data sources: NTFA strategic classification schema, Ministry of Industry and Trade sectoral data, university finance departments, and partnership registries.

A.2 Normalization and Aggregation

All raw indicators are normalized to a 0–100 scale using min-max normalization relative to a reference group of upper-middle-income and advanced small-economy comparators (e.g., South Korea 1990, Singapore 2000, Ireland 2010, Malaysia 2020). The composite indices are then computed as simple arithmetic means of their normalized indicators:

· E_index = (E1 + E2 + E3 + E4) / 4
· R_index = (R1 + R2 + R3 + R4 + R5) / 5
· P_index = (P1 + P2 + P3 + P4) / 4
· A_index = (A1 + A2 + A3 + A4) / 4

The overall NSCI is then the product NSCI = E_index × R_index × P_index × A_index, mirroring the multiplicative CCM structure, yielding a theoretical maximum of 100⁴ = 100,000,000, which can be rescaled for practical presentation (e.g., log-transformed or divided by 10⁶).

A.3 Calibration for Morocco Baseline (2025 Estimate)

Applying available proxy data and expert elicitation, the estimated Morocco baseline indices are:

Variable Indicator Scores (estimated) Composite Index
E E1=45, E2=30, E3=40, E4=35 37.5
R R1=15, R2=5, R3=20, R4=8, R5=20 13.6
P P1=30, P2=25, P3=35, P4=40 32.5
A A1=25, A2=15, A3=10, A4=20 17.5

NSCI (raw product) = 37.5 × 13.6 × 32.5 × 17.5 ≈ 288,750 (rescaled: 0.29 on a log-adjusted scale where 1.0 represents the threshold for strategic sovereignty).

This extremely low R_index and A_index, combined with moderate E and P, confirms the catastrophic suppression of T_eff predicted by the multiplicative model.

