APPENDIX B — RFUA MISSION LABORATORY DESIGN TEMPLATES

This appendix provides operational blueprints for five founding Mission Laboratories, each aligned to a Moroccan national strategic priority sector. These templates are intended as starting points for institutional design and can be adapted to specific university contexts.

B.1 Moroccan AI and Autonomous Systems Lab

Mission Statement: To develop sovereign Moroccan capacity in artificial intelligence and autonomous systems for defense, logistics, and industrial automation, training engineers capable of designing and deploying original AI architectures rather than adapting imported solutions.

Research Areas: Natural language processing for Arabic and Amazigh; computer vision for agricultural and urban monitoring; reinforcement learning for industrial robotics; AI ethics and regulatory frameworks for North African contexts; edge AI for remote infrastructure.

Required Faculty Expertise: Machine learning, computer vision, robotics, computational linguistics (Arabic NLP), systems engineering, AI policy/ethics.

Infrastructure Needs: High-performance GPU cluster, robotics prototyping lab, drone test range, secure data sandboxes for government/defense applications.

Industry & Government Partners: Ministry of Defense (cyber command), OCP Group (automated mining and logistics), Royal Air Maroc (predictive maintenance), National Police (smart surveillance), Ministry of Agriculture (precision farming).

Student Integration Plan: Year 1–2: foundational AI and mathematics coursework. Year 3: team-based project contributing to a lab research stream (e.g., improving an existing Arabic speech recognition model). Year 4: individual thesis project embedded in a partner use case. Graduate: co-authored publication and deployed prototype expected.

Expected Outputs (5-year horizon): 200 AI/ML-specialized graduates, 50 peer-reviewed publications, 5 deployed national-scale AI systems, 3 spin-off startups.

B.2 Aerospace Structures and Propulsion Lab

Mission Statement: To support Morocco’s ambition to manufacture a complete aircraft by 2030 by developing indigenous expertise in aerospace structural design, composite materials manufacturing, and propulsion system integration.

Research Areas: Lightweight composite structures; additive manufacturing for aerospace components; thermomechanical simulation; propulsion system MRO; avionics integration; drone and urban air mobility platforms.

Required Faculty Expertise: Aerospace engineering, mechanical engineering, materials science, computational fluid dynamics, avionics.

Infrastructure Needs: Composite materials fabrication and testing facility, wind tunnel, 3D metal printers, structural test rigs, aircraft systems integration mockup.

Industry & Government Partners: Boeing Morocco, Safran Aircraft Engines, Spirit AeroSystems, Royal Moroccan Air Force, ONDA (airport authority).

Student Integration Plan: Students contribute to real component design and testing cycles from Year 3 onward, with a capstone project that delivers a physical prototype (e.g., a drone wing spar made from Moroccan-sourced composites) tested to industry standards.

B.3 Advanced Manufacturing and Robotics Lab

Mission Statement: To create the engineering talent pool required to sustain and upgrade Morocco’s automotive and advanced manufacturing sectors, moving from assembly-line operations to factory-of-the-future design and implementation.

Research Areas: Industrial robotics and cobotics; digital twin simulation for production lines; predictive maintenance algorithms; flexible manufacturing systems; supply chain AI; mechatronics.

Required Faculty Expertise: Mechatronics, industrial engineering, computer science (IoT, edge computing), mechanical engineering, operations research.

Infrastructure Needs: Industry 4.0 demonstration cell, collaborative robot fleet, PLC and SCADA training systems, digital twin software suite, sensor lab.

Industry & Government Partners: Renault-Nissan Tanger, Stellantis Kenitra, AMICA, SOMACA, OCP industrial operations, Schneider Electric Morocco.

Student Integration Plan: Direct industry immersion from Year 3, with students solving real production line problems identified by partner firms and delivering implementations measured by cost savings or quality improvement.

B.4 Saharan Solar Energy Systems Lab

Mission Statement: To make Morocco a global hub for concentrated solar power and green hydrogen production technology, building indigenous capacity to design, optimize, and export energy systems suited to desert environments.

Research Areas: High-temperature thermal storage; heliostat field optimization; solar-to-hydrogen conversion pathways; hybrid CSP-PV systems; water desalination powered by solar thermal; energy grid integration.

Required Faculty Expertise: Thermal engineering, materials science (corrosion, high-temperature), electrical engineering (power electronics, grid integration), chemical engineering (hydrogen, electrolysis), atmospheric science (soiling and dust mitigation).

Infrastructure Needs: Small-scale CSP test loop, solar simulator, advanced materials characterization lab, hydrogen electrolysis test station, outdoor test site (possible location: Ouarzazate Noor complex).

Industry & Government Partners: MASEN, ONEE, IRESEN, OCP Green Energy, international CSP research institutes (DLR Germany, NREL USA).

Student Integration Plan: Students rotate through Noor station operations in Years 2–3, then conduct thesis projects on component-level improvements with joint supervision from MASEN engineers.

B.5 National Water Systems Engineering Lab

Mission Statement: To address Morocco’s water security challenge — one of the most acute in the Mediterranean region — through advanced engineering of water management, treatment, and reuse systems.

Research Areas: Membrane-based desalination; wastewater treatment and resource recovery; smart irrigation systems; groundwater modeling and managed aquifer recharge; climate resilience of water infrastructure.

Required Faculty Expertise: Environmental engineering, hydrological modeling, chemical engineering, sensor networks, civil engineering.

Infrastructure Needs: Bench-scale desalination test beds, water quality analysis lab, hydrological modeling software suite, IoT sensor network testbed.

Industry & Government Partners: ONEE Water Branch, Ministry of Water and Equipment, Regional Agricultural Development Offices (ORMVA), OCP water treatment division.

Student Integration Plan: Immersion projects embedded in regional water agencies, designing improvements to local treatment plants or irrigation networks, with outcomes measured in terms of water savings or quality improvements.

