APPENDIX C — AATFS TECHNICAL ARCHITECTURE SPECIFICATIONS

This appendix describes the conceptual technical architecture of the AI-Augmented Talent Formation System (AATFS). It outlines the four core subsystems, their data flows, AI model families, and integration requirements, providing a foundation for future technical development.

C.1 System Overview

The AATFS is a distributed, cloud-native platform integrating with university learning management systems, national research databases, and industry partner systems. It consists of four interoperating modules that together form a continuous intelligence layer for talent formation.

C.2 Personalized Competence Mapping (PCM) Engine

Function: Continuously ingests multimodal evidence of student learning and performance, constructs a dynamic Competence Vector, and provides adaptive feedback to students and educators.

Input Data Streams:

· Digital learning interactions (LMS logs: time on task, problem-solving sequences, error patterns, help-seeking behaviors)
· Project deliverables (code repositories, design documents, simulation outputs)
· Peer and mentor evaluations (structured rubrics)
· Assessment results (both conventional and scenario-based)
· Self-reported confidence and metacognitive reflections

AI Architecture:

· A Bayesian knowledge tracing framework augmented with transformer-based encoders that embed unstructured artifacts (code, writing) into a shared representation space.
· A temporal graph neural network models skill acquisition trajectories, capturing dependencies between competencies.
· Output is a vector of mastery probabilities across ~500 granular competencies, updated nightly.

Output: A Competence Dashboard for each student and aggregated anonymized views for faculty and national administrators. Alerts generated when a student’s growth trajectory deviates from expected norms.

C.3 Research Mentor Intelligence (RMI) System

Function: Optimizes matching of students to mentors, Mission Lab projects, and national strategic priorities.

Data Model:

· Student Node: Competence Vector + stated interests + career aspirations + location/time constraints.
· Mentor Node: Research domain expertise (topic model of publications), mentorship capacity (current load), mentorship style (survey-based), funding availability.
· Project Node: Required competencies, strategic alignment score (from NKG), timeline, team composition.

Matching Algorithm:

· A multi-objective recommendation system combining collaborative filtering and constraint satisfaction.
· Initially computes a compatibility score using cosine similarity between student Competence Vector and project requirement vector.
· Then adjusts for mentorship load balancing, strategic priority weighting (national missions get boosted scores), and diversity objectives (ensuring cross-disciplinary teams).
· Final recommendations are presented to human coordinators for approval, with an explainability layer providing natural-language justification.

Feedback Loop: Outcomes of matches (project success, student satisfaction, publication output) are fed back to retrain the matching model, continuously improving precision.

C.4 Simulation-Based Engineering Laboratory (SBEL) Platform

Function: Provides scalable, AI-augmented virtual engineering environments.

Core Capabilities:

· Physics Engine Integration: Couples with high-fidelity simulation tools (ANSYS, COMSOL, Gazebo, MATLAB/Simulink) via API, abstracting complexity behind task-specific interfaces.
· AI Experimentation Agent: An LLM-based assistant that helps students formulate hypotheses, design simulation experiments, interpret results, and suggest next steps — acting as a junior lab supervisor.
· Collaborative Mode: Multi-user virtual labs where students can co-design and test systems asynchronously.
· Failure Injection: Configurable scenarios that introduce realistic faults (sensor noise, material defects, network latency) to train diagnostic skills.

Technical Requirements: Containerized simulation environments orchestrated via Kubernetes, GPU-enabled for physics acceleration where applicable, integrated with institutional single sign-on and student data records.

C.5 National Knowledge Graph (NKG)

Function: A system-level strategic intelligence layer mapping the relationships between talent, research, industry needs, and global knowledge frontiers.

Ontology: Core entity types include: Institution, Researcher, Student, Publication, Patent, Project, Discipline, StrategicSector, IndustrialPartner, NationalMission, FundingStream. Relationships include: produces, employs, contributesTo, cites, requires, alignedWith.

Data Sources:

· Internal: University research information systems, NTFA databases, student records, NSCI data.
· External: Scopus, WIPO, Global Innovation Index, industry association reports, job posting aggregators.
· Diaspora: Web scraping of LinkedIn, ORCID, and academic institutional pages to map Moroccan researchers abroad.

Graph Construction: An automated ingestion pipeline extracts entities and relations using named entity recognition and relation extraction models fine-tuned on academic and policy text. A graph database (e.g., Neo4j) stores the resulting knowledge graph.

Query Capabilities:

· "Show the competence supply-demand gap for mechatronics in the automotive sector over the next 5 years."
· "Identify the top five Moroccan diaspora researchers in solid-state batteries who have recently co-authored with industry partners."
· "Recommend the optimal location for a new Mission Lab in hydrogen metallurgy based on industry concentration, existing university strengths, and infrastructure."

Access Control: Role-based, with strategic queries available to NTFA and government planning bodies, while anonymized aggregate data feeds public dashboards.

