APPENDIX E — DIASPORA RESEARCHER RECRUITMENT FRAMEWORK

This appendix provides a strategic framework for identifying, engaging, and repatriating Moroccan research talent from the diaspora as part of the RFUA faculty architecture. The framework is designed to convert the current brain drain dynamic into a brain gain pipeline.

E.1 Strategic Rationale

Morocco possesses a substantial and highly skilled diaspora concentrated in France, Canada, Spain, the United States, Germany, and the Gulf. Estimates suggest thousands of Moroccan-born PhD holders and senior engineers work in academia and technology firms globally. Ambassador Youssef Amrani has stated that Moroccan expatriates represent "a strategic asset, capable of mentoring, co-investing, and supporting projects that connect skills and opportunities across continents."

The RFUA’s demand for internationally competitive, research-active faculty cannot be met solely through domestic PhD production in the short to medium term. Therefore, a proactive diaspora recruitment strategy is a critical enabler of Phase 1 and Phase 2 institutional development.

E.2 Operational Phases

Phase A: Identification and Mapping (Ongoing, initiated Year 0)

· Leverage the National Knowledge Graph (NKG) to scan global publication databases (Scopus, Web of Science), patent databases, and professional networks (LinkedIn, ORCID) to identify Moroccan-affiliated researchers in strategic domains.
· Establish a "Diaspora Talent Database" maintained by the NTFA, categorizing individuals by field, career stage (postdoc, early-career PI, senior scientist), institutional affiliation, and expressed or inferred openness to relocation.
· Collaborate with Moroccan embassies and consulates to organize "Diaspora Talent Mapping Missions" — events that gather data on diaspora professionals’ career trajectories and aspirations.

Phase B: Engagement and Network Building (Years 1–3)

· Host annual "Morocco Future Frontiers" symposia in key diaspora hubs (Paris, Montreal, Washington D.C., Berlin), featuring high-level government participation and showcasing national strategic missions (e.g., "Design the Next Generation of Saharan Solar" or "Build the Moroccan AI for Health Platform"). These events serve as networking platforms and soft recruitment tools.
· Create a "Virtual Morocco Lab" portal — an online platform where diaspora researchers can collaborate remotely with Moroccan Mission Labs on short-term projects, co-supervise PhD students, or deliver guest lectures, lowering the barrier to initial engagement.
· Establish a "Diaspora Sabbatical Fund" offering generous stipends for senior researchers to spend 3–12 months embedded in Moroccan RFUA institutions, with full relocation support, to experience the research environment and mission alignment first-hand.

Phase C: Repatriation Incentive Package (Years 2–8)

Design a competitive incentive structure to make relocation attractive, recognizing that compensation alone will not suffice; mission, autonomy, and family considerations are paramount.

· Competitive Compensation: Salaries benchmarked against European Union academic scales for equivalent positions, supplemented by tax incentives (e.g., a five-year income tax holiday for returning diaspora researchers, modeled on existing investment charter incentives).
· Research Autonomy and Start-Up Packages: Guaranteed start-up research funding (€200,000–500,000 depending on field and seniority) for laboratory equipment, PhD studentships, and seed grants, with minimal bureaucratic overhead for the first three years.
· National Mission Affiliation: Each recruited researcher is appointed to lead or co-lead a Mission Lab directly linked to a high-impact national project, providing intrinsic motivation and professional prominence.
· Dual-Career and Family Support: Employment assistance for spouses (coordinated with national and international companies), international school placements for children, and concierge-style relocation services to handle housing, visas, and administrative procedures.
· "Return Bridge" Tenure: For researchers hesitant to fully commit, offer joint appointments with a partner university abroad, allowing 50/50 time splits for an initial period, gradually transitioning to full-time in Morocco.

Phase D: Reintegration and Retention (Ongoing)

· Establish a peer support network of repatriated researchers to share experiences and problem-solve reintegration challenges.
· Monitor retention through annual check-ins and adjust incentive structures based on feedback.
· Celebrate repatriated researchers as national heroes through media campaigns, reinforcing the prestige of contributing to national sovereignty.

